H('Abstract',1)
para(f'Microscopic simulation of rapidly motorising cities is often limited by the absence of public demand data rather than by software. Two recent SUMO/TraCI studies of a Riyadh arterial corridor evaluated autonomous-vehicle (AV) behaviour but released neither traffic counts nor origin–destination (OD) data. This study builds and stress-tests an open alternative: an OpenStreetMap-derived network, a seeded synthetic demand of 1,800 trips and a TraCI logger of vehicle speed and position at 0.5 s. An audit shows that geometry is obtainable but observed demand is not, and that the extract is fragmented into seven connected components, including a 13.5 km primary edge no trip can use. The baseline (mean travel time {T.loc["duration","mean"]:.1f} s) is stable across ten seeds (CV {p2.std(ddof=1)/p2.mean()*100:.1f}%), but halving the insertion period from 2 s to 1 s causes sustained gridlock in {nTele} of {nT} seeds (ten-seed mean {p1.mean():.0f} s), which a single seed would have missed. Static signals at five hotspot junctions reduce the gridlock-regime mean from {TLS[(TLS["var"]=="none")&(TLS.p==1)].dur.mean():.0f} s to {TLS[(TLS["var"]=="hot5")&(TLS.p==1)].dur.mean():.0f} s but add {TLS[(TLS["var"]=="hot5")&(TLS.p==2)].dur.mean()-TLS[(TLS["var"]=="none")&(TLS.p==2)].dur.mean():.0f} s at lighter demand. AV shares of 0–50% lower time loss by {tl_red2:.0f}–{tl_red15:.0f}% in stable regimes without changing indicative CO2 per kilometre. In a twin experiment, route-sampler calibration with counts alone did worse than no calibration (held-out GEH<5: {fr.loc[0.05,"val"]*100:.0f}% at 5% station coverage, {fr.loc[0.5,"val"]*100:.0f}% at 50%, versus {UNC["geh_lt5"]*100:.0f}% uncalibrated); adding known hourly totals restored it to {frt.loc[0.05,"val"]*100:.0f}%. The demand is uncalibrated and the extract covers the Al Janadiriyah Road and an adjoining residential grid, not the source corridor, so the results form a verified baseline and workflow rather than a calibrated Riyadh model.')
P_('Keywords: SUMO; TraCI; synthetic traffic demand; OpenStreetMap; Riyadh; microscopic simulation; sensitivity analysis; automated vehicles; calibration; reproducibility',ital=True,sp=10,sz=10)
H('Highlights',1)
bullets(['Open SUMO/TraCI baseline for Riyadh with a 336,115-record trajectory log at 0.5 s.',
 f'Halving the insertion period (2 s to 1 s) triggers gridlock in {nTele} of {nT} seeds; single-seed results mislead.',
 'The OSM extract is fragmented: seven connected components and a 13.5 km unusable primary edge.',
 f'AV shares up to 50% cut time loss by about {tl_red2:.0f}–{tl_red15:.0f}% but not CO2 per km in stable regimes.',
 'Count-only route-sampler calibration can underperform no calibration; adding total demand helps.'])
# ------------ 1 INTRO
H('1. Introduction',1)
para('Riyadh is among the largest and fastest-growing capitals in the Middle East, and its road network is dominated by private passenger cars. Planning for connected and automated vehicles, electrification and congestion management in such a setting depends on simulation, because field trials of new vehicle technologies are costly and, for automation, still legally and operationally constrained. Open-source microscopic simulators have become the default instrument for this work. Among them, Eclipse SUMO [1,13], coupled with the Traffic Control Interface (TraCI) [2], allows a script to read and modify the state of individual vehicles while a simulation runs, which makes it suitable for emulating communication-dependent behaviour without a dedicated network simulator.')
para('Two studies by Louati and co-authors apply this combination to a single arterial corridor in Riyadh. The first [10] approximates vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V), vehicle-to-infrastructure (V2I) and vehicle-to-network (V2N) behaviour through TraCI and reports travel-time, stop-frequency, speed and CO2 outcomes at AV penetrations of 0%, 10%, 30% and 50%. The second [11] monetises the external costs of autonomous versus human-driven battery-electric vehicles using a cost framework adapted from a German study [12], and employs simulation to check its congestion assumptions. Both are valuable, but both leave an outsider unable to rebuild the demand side of the model: hourly link volumes, turning movements and the OD structure are not provided in a form that can be loaded into a simulator.')
para('This gap is common. Well-documented open SUMO scenarios exist for Luxembourg [5], Bologna [6] and Ingolstadt [7], and they are cited precisely because most city studies cannot assemble comparable demand. Where counts are missing, practitioners fall back on stochastic trip generators. The consequences of that fallback, namely how sensitive the headline indicators are to its few parameters, how much run-to-run noise it adds, and whether the later steps of a study (signal design, AV scenarios, emission estimates, calibration) behave sensibly on top of it, are rarely reported, even though they determine how much any later comparison can be trusted.')
para('The present paper addresses that question on a network that anyone can regenerate. Its contributions are: (i) a structured audit of the demand inputs a SUMO study needs against what Riyadh public portals and commercial indices provide; (ii) a fully specified, seeded synthetic-demand baseline with a headless TraCI trajectory logger and a connectivity audit of the extracted network; (iii) a quantified description of the baseline, including per-vehicle distributions, junction-level delay hot spots and a flow–density analysis extended by an arterial stress test; (iv) ten-seed sensitivity analyses of insertion rate and fringe weighting that expose a bifurcation into sustained gridlock which single-seed runs fail to reveal; (v) controlled extensions that add static signal control, a heterogeneous fleet with an AV share and indicative emissions, and a time-varying demand profile; (vi) a twin experiment that tests whether count-based calibration with SUMO\'s route sampler recovers a known demand and how many counting stations it needs; (vii) a cross-version reproducibility test; and (viii) an explicit audit of the accompanying public repository. To our knowledge no earlier work reports this combination for a Riyadh network, although we do not claim an exhaustive search of the grey literature.')
para('The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 states the problem and research questions. Section 4 presents the data audit. Section 5 describes the methodology. Section 6 reports results, Section 7 discusses them, Section 8 treats limitations and how each was addressed, and Section 9 concludes.')
# ------------ 2 RELATED
H('2. Related work',1)
H('2.1 SUMO and TraCI in connected- and automated-vehicle studies',2)
para('SUMO is a continuous-space, discrete-time microscopic simulator whose default longitudinal model derives from the safe-speed formulation of Krauß and colleagues [3], with an alternative family that includes the Intelligent Driver Model [4]. Its lane-changing and junction logic, the tooling for importing OpenStreetMap data, and the plain-XML input formats are described in the project overview [1,13]. TraCI, introduced to couple traffic and network simulators [2], exposes vehicle, edge, junction and signal state through a client–server socket. In AV and V2X research it is the usual route for emulating cooperative behaviour: the script observes neighbours, then overrides speed, headway or lane choice. Paper [10] follows this pattern, mapping automated driving to tightened car-following parameters (a short desired time headway and small minimum gap) and driving cooperative responses through TraCI calls. Such emulation is an approximation of vehicle dynamics and communication, and its credibility rests on the baseline against which it is compared, which is the reason this paper concentrates on the baseline.')
H('2.2 Open scenarios and demand synthesis',2)
para('Scenario libraries show how much effort realistic demand requires. The Luxembourg scenario [5] provides a 24-hour demand profile for vehicular-networking research. The Bologna scenario [6] was assembled from open data and municipal sources. InTAS for Ingolstadt [7] combines traffic counts, signal information and public transport with a calibration step. These efforts share a dependence on local measurements, which are exactly what is unavailable for many cities. When measurements are absent, SUMO offers randomTrips.py, which draws origin and destination edges at random, optionally weighting border (fringe) edges, and a routing step that converts the trips to routes [14]. For calibration against observations, routeSampler.py selects routes from a candidate set to match edge counts, turning counts or OD relations [14]. The first tool needs nothing beyond a network; the second needs data. Our study positions the first as a transparent placeholder, quantifies what is lost by using it, and tests the second on a case where the true demand is known.')
H('2.3 Calibration and validation practice',2)
para('Guidance for applying microsimulation, such as the Federal Highway Administration toolbox [8], recommends comparing simulated and observed volumes with the GEH statistic and speeds or travel times with measured values, and treats uncalibrated models as unsuitable for decisions. A widely used acceptance rule requires GEH below 5 for most counting locations. Because randomTrips.py output is by construction uncalibrated, any use of it must be accompanied by an explicit statement of that status, and any comparative claim must be bounded by the variability the generator itself introduces. Two sources of variability are relevant: the generator parameters, and the random seed that fixes both trip sampling and stochastic driver behaviour. Seed-ensemble reporting is routine in other fields but is often missing from SUMO case studies, and the results below show why it matters.')
H('2.4 Road-network data from OpenStreetMap',2)
para('OpenStreetMap (OSM) is a convenient source of network geometry, lane counts and speed limits, and it is licensed under the Open Database Licence [15]. Its completeness and attribute quality vary by place and tag [9]. Features that matter to traffic simulation, such as signal locations, lane-level turn restrictions and priority rules, are inconsistently mapped. A network converted automatically from OSM therefore inherits the mapping choices of local contributors, and its fidelity should be checked rather than assumed. Graph connectivity is a basic check of this kind: a rectangular extract that clips one-way roads can leave edges or components that trips cannot reach.')
H('2.5 Studies of Riyadh and the identified gap',2)
para('The two Riyadh studies [10,11] are the closest precedents. They share a corridor but use different demand datasets and vehicle assumptions, and neither makes its counts or OD structure available. Related work on the economics of automated vehicles [12] supplies the cost framework adapted in [11]. What is missing is an open, repeatable baseline for Riyadh that states what the public data can and cannot support, shows the behaviour of the stop-gap demand generator, demonstrates the steps that would follow on a calibrated model (signals, AV scenarios, emissions, time-of-day demand) and provides trajectory-level instrumentation ready for calibration. That is the contribution of this paper.')
# ------------ 3 PROBLEM
H('3. Problem statement and research questions',1)
para('Let G=(N,E) be a road network derived from OSM and let D be a demand specification (a set of timed origin–destination trips). A simulation study reports indicators I(G,D,θ,s) that depend on model parameters θ and a random seed s. When D is not observed, it must be generated from a stochastic procedure with parameters φ, so the reported indicators acquire a dependence I(G,D(φ,s),θ,s) that is rarely characterised. The problem addressed here is to determine how large that dependence is for a Riyadh network, how it should be reported, how it interacts with downstream modelling choices, and which parts of the demand specification can be replaced by public data in the future.')
para('We pose eight research questions. RQ1: Which of the demand inputs required by SUMO can be obtained from Riyadh public sources? RQ2: What baseline conditions result from a seeded synthetic demand on the OSM-derived network, and is the network structurally sound? RQ3: How sensitive are the aggregate indicators to the insertion period and fringe weighting, and is the response smooth? RQ4: How much indicator variability is due to the random seed alone? RQ5: How do static signal control and a heterogeneous fleet with an AV share change delay and indicative emissions? RQ6: How does a time-varying demand profile change the picture relative to a uniform hour? RQ7: Would count-based calibration with the route sampler recover a known demand, and how many counting stations would it need? RQ8: Are the results reproducible across SUMO versions and from the public repository?')
# ------------ 4 AUDIT
H('4. Data-availability audit',1)
para('The audit lists each demand-side input a SUMO study requires, the Riyadh sources identified for it, and whether data of the required form were retrieved. The audit was conducted by the author between August and October 2026 using the portals named in Table 1, and it records what was found, not what may exist behind access requests. Entries marked "not retrieved" mean that no downloadable dataset at the necessary spatial and temporal resolution was located; they do not imply that such data do not exist.')
