fig('fig/f2_demand.png','Demand profile (departures per five minutes) and number of vehicles in the network. The uniform insertion produces a quasi-stationary occupancy of roughly 50 to 60 vehicles after the first few minutes.')
fig('fig/f3_dist.png','Distributions of route length, travel time, time loss and mean trip speed (vertical orange line: median). Travel time is almost proportional to route length (Pearson r = 0.91).',w=6.5)
H('6.4 Trajectory-level results from TraCI',2)
para('Of the 336,115 vehicle-step samples, 8,154 (2.43%) were below the 2 m/s threshold. These samples do not mainly reflect vehicle insertion: only 342 occurred within ten seconds of a vehicle first appearing and only 18 within three seconds of leaving, leaving 7,794 mid-trip samples (2.32% of all samples) affecting 395 vehicles (21.9%). The delay is strongly concentrated. Two residential edges, -1095828493#0 (45 m) and -278305873#2 (202 m), account for 40.4% of all slow samples, and 24.5% of the mid-trip samples lie on internal junction lanes. No slow sample was recorded on any of the six primary edges (Figure 6). Because there are no signals, these slowdowns arise from priority and right-before-left conflicts at junctions of the residential grid, together with queues for shared downstream edges.')
fig('fig/f4_speed.png','Distribution of instantaneous speeds in the TraCI log. The majority of samples are at or near the residential speed limit of 13.89 m/s; a secondary mass near zero corresponds to junction interactions.',w=4.4)
fig('fig/f5_slowmap.png','Location of slow-speed samples (<2 m/s) by edge, logarithmic colour scale. Delay is confined to a few approaches of the residential grid; the arterial is free-flowing.')
table('Edges with most slow-speed samples (mid-trip and insertion combined).',['Edge ID','Type','Length (m)','Slow samples','Share of all'],[
['-1095828493#0','Residential','45','1,874','23.0%'],['-278305873#2','Residential','202','1,420','17.4%'],['278305900','Residential','86','243','3.0%'],['278305878#1','Residential','128','213','2.6%'],['-889249672','Unclassified','98','200','2.5%']],widths=[1.8,1.2,1.0,1.2,1.0],note='Share relative to 8,154 slow-speed samples. Internal junction lanes are not listed.')
H('6.5 Flow–density behaviour',2)
para('Edie-type observations for the baseline were obtained for 2,483 edge–window pairs (Figure 7). Density never exceeded 21.3 vehicles per kilometre per lane and flow never exceeded 448 vehicles per hour per lane, which is well below commonly cited urban lane capacities of roughly 1,500 to 2,000 vehicles per hour. The baseline cloud therefore samples only the free-flow branch and the onset of interaction at junction approaches.')
fig('fig/f6_fd.png','Flow–density and speed–density observations per edge and 300 s window, normalised per lane. All baseline observations lie in the uncongested regime.')
para(f'The arterial stress test (Experiment G) extends the diagram towards capacity. On the isolated 13.5 km edge, where no junction interferes, per-lane flow rose almost linearly with density to {lone.q.max():.0f} veh/h/lane at {lone.k[lone.q.idxmax()]:.1f} veh/km/lane, while the space-mean speed declined from {lone.speed.max()*3.6:.0f} to {lone.speed.min()*3.6:.0f} km/h (Figure 8). No breakdown to a congested branch occurred up to the largest flow, so the highest observed flow is a lower bound on the capacity under the default car-following parameters. On the connected chain the highest observed flow on its middle edge was {chain.q.max():.0f} veh/h/lane at a density of {chain.k[chain.q.idxmax()]:.1f} veh/km/lane. These figures give the simulator\'s own intrinsic fundamental diagram, which a calibration to Riyadh counts and speeds would adjust through the car-following parameters.')
fig('fig/f16_arterial.png','Flow–density and speed–density observations from the arterial stress test, per lane and 300 s window. The isolated edge approaches capacity without breakdown; the connected chain is shown for comparison.',w=6.2)
H('6.6 Sensitivity to demand intensity: a gridlock bifurcation',2)
rowsA=[[f'{p:g}',int(S[(S.p==p)&(S.ff==10)].n.iloc[0]),pm(sg(p),1),pm(sg(p,c="wait"),2),pm(sg(p,c="tl"),1),pm(sg(p,c="spd"),2),f'{np.mean(sg(p,c="maxrun")):.0f}'] for p in [10.0,5.0,3.0,2.0,1.5,1.0]]
table('Experiment A: effect of the insertion period (fringe factor 10; mean ± 95% interval over 10 seeds).',['p (s)','Vehicles','Travel time (s)','Waiting time (s)','Time loss (s)','Trip speed (m/s)','Peak vehicles'],rowsA,widths=[0.6,0.8,1.2,1.2,1.1,1.1,0.9])
para(f'From p = 10 s down to p = 1.5 s the response is smooth and small: mean travel time rises from {sg(10.0).mean():.1f} s to {sg(1.5).mean():.1f} s, and the ten-seed intervals are within 1 to 2 s. At p = 1 s the system behaves differently. The mean travel time is {p1.mean():.0f} s with a 95% interval of ±{ci(p1):.0f} s and a standard deviation of {p1.std(ddof=1):.0f} s, about 300 times the seed-to-seed standard deviation at p = 1.5 s ({p15.std(ddof=1):.2f} s). Individual seeds range from {p1.min():.0f} s to {p1.max():.0f} s. Seven of ten seeds produced sustained jams in which SUMO teleported blocked vehicles [20], and across the ten seeds the network took between {GL.end.min()/60:.0f} and {GL.end.max()/60:.0f} minutes to clear (Figure 10). The remaining three seeds recovered without teleporting. The single-seed run of the baseline generator at p = 1 s (seed 42) gave a mean travel time of 125 s, a 34% increase over p = 2 s that would have suggested a moderate transition; its magnitude resembles the benign seeds. The demand level at which the network is marginal is therefore between 2,400 and 3,600 vehicles per hour, and below it the indicators are stable while above it they are dominated by whether a local jam locks.')
