h2=TLS[(TLS['var']=='hot5')&(TLS.p==2)]; n2=TLS[(TLS['var']=='none')&(TLS.p==2)]; a2=TLS[(TLS['var']=='all')&(TLS.p==2)]; h1=TLS[(TLS['var']=='hot5')&(TLS.p==1)]; n1=TLS[(TLS['var']=='none')&(TLS.p==1)]; a1=TLS[(TLS['var']=='all')&(TLS.p==1)]
para(f'The effect of signals depends on the demand regime. At p = 2 s, where the unsignalised network flows freely, default static plans increase mean travel time from {n2.dur.mean():.1f} s to {h2.dur.mean():.1f} s with five signalised hotspots (+{(h2.dur.mean()/n2.dur.mean()-1)*100:.0f}%) and to {a2.dur.mean():.1f} s with 61 signalised junctions (+{(a2.dur.mean()/n2.dur.mean()-1)*100:.0f}%), because untuned cycles impose waiting on vehicles that would otherwise find gaps. At p = 1 s, where unsignalised runs lock in seven of ten seeds, five hotspot signals lower the mean travel time from {n1.dur.mean():.0f} s to {h1.dur.mean():.0f} s and its seed-to-seed standard deviation from {n1.dur.std(ddof=1):.0f} s to {h1.dur.std(ddof=1):.0f} s, whereas signalising 61 junctions gives {a1.dur.mean():.0f} s. The pattern is consistent with signals resolving priority conflicts that otherwise deadlock, at the cost of delay at light demand, and suggests that the benefit depends on the number and timing of controlled junctions. Targeted placement guided by trajectory hot spots is clearly preferable to blanket signalisation in this network, and signal timing is a first-order unknown that real plans from the traffic authority would settle.')
