para(f'In the two stable regimes the AV share has a small but consistent effect on delay. At p = 2 s, raising the AV share from 0 to 50% lowers mean time loss from {av(2.0,0,"tl").mean():.1f} s to {av(2.0,50,"tl").mean():.1f} s ({tl_red2:.0f}%; Welch p = {pv_tl2:.1g}) and mean travel time by {tt_red2:.1f}%; at p = 1.5 s the reductions are {tl_red15:.0f}% in time loss and {tt_red15:.1f}% in travel time. Indicative CO2 per kilometre does not change ({co2d2:+.1f}% at p = 2 s), which is consistent with a mostly uncongested network in which all vehicle types share one powertrain. At p = 1 s the ensemble is dominated by the gridlock bifurcation of Section 6.6, the intervals overlap widely and no ordering by AV share can be claimed. The lesson for AV studies is that a benefit can only be measured where a baseline is stable, and that reporting at several demand levels is necessary.')
