H('6.11 Calibration-readiness twin experiment',2)
para(f'The observed demand in the twin experiment has {int(E5["truth_n"])} vehicles and a mean travel time of {E5["truth_mean_tt"]:.0f} s. Counts exist on {n_cnt_edges} edges. The uncalibrated uniform comparator, which has the same hourly totals but no OD concentration, reproduces hourly counts on {UNC["geh_lt5"]*100:.0f}% of the {UNC["n"]} edge-hours with GEH below 5 (mean GEH {UNC["geh_mean"]:.1f}, r = {UNC["r"]:.2f}). Calibrating with counts only gives held-out GEH<5 shares of {fr.loc[0.05,"val"]*100:.0f}%, {fr.loc[0.1,"val"]*100:.0f}%, {fr.loc[0.25,"val"]*100:.0f}% and {fr.loc[0.5,"val"]*100:.0f}% at 5, 10, 25 and 50% station coverage (Table 15, Figure 15): every level is below the uncalibrated comparator, although agreement improves steadily with coverage (held-out r from {fr.loc[0.05,"r"]:.2f} to {fr.loc[0.5,"r"]:.2f}). On the stations used for fitting, GEH<5 holds for {fr.loc[0.5,"fit"]*100:.0f}% to {fr.loc[0.05,"fit"]*100:.0f}% of edge-hours, so fitted-location agreement overstates quality, and the gap to held-out locations measures over-fitting. A large part of the problem is that counts alone do not fix the total demand: the number of calibrated vehicles ranged from {fr.nv.min():.0f} to {fr.nv.max():.0f} on average across coverage levels against 3,600 in the truth. When the known hourly totals are added, held-out GEH<5 becomes {frt.loc[0.05,"val"]*100:.0f}%, {frt.loc[0.1,"val"]*100:.0f}%, {frt.loc[0.25,"val"]*100:.0f}% and {frt.loc[0.5,"val"]*100:.0f}% (mean GEH {frt.loc[0.05,"gm"]:.1f} to {frt.loc[0.5,"gm"]:.1f}), which is roughly the level of the uncalibrated comparator: in this twin sparse counts added little beyond the correct total, and only the highest coverage exceeded it. None of these configurations reaches the 85% acceptance level commonly cited for microsimulation [8]. The implication for a real calibration is that counts from a handful of stations are unlikely to yield an acceptable model; that an independent estimate of total demand is as valuable as additional stations; and that the held-out check must be reported, not just the fit.')
rowsC=[[f'{int(f*100)}%',f'{fr.loc[f,"ns"]:.0f}','counts only',f'{fr.loc[f,"nv"]:.0f}',f'{fr.loc[f,"fit"]*100:.0f}%',f'{fr.loc[f,"val"]*100:.0f}% ± {fr.loc[f,"valsd"]*100:.0f}',f'{fr.loc[f,"gm"]:.1f}',f'{fr.loc[f,"r"]:.2f}'] for f in [0.05,0.1,0.25,0.5]]
rowsC+=[[f'{int(f*100)}%',f'{fr.loc[f,"ns"]:.0f}','counts + totals',f'{frt.loc[f,"nv"]:.0f}',f'{frt.loc[f,"fit"]*100:.0f}%',f'{frt.loc[f,"val"]*100:.0f}% ± {frt.loc[f,"valsd"]*100:.0f}',f'{frt.loc[f,"gm"]:.1f}',f'{frt.loc[f,"r"]:.2f}'] for f in [0.05,0.1,0.25,0.5]]
rowsC.append(['Uncalibrated','—','uniform OD','3600','—',f'{UNC["geh_lt5"]*100:.0f}%',f'{UNC["geh_mean"]:.1f}',f'{UNC["r"]:.2f}'])
table('Experiment F: calibration of the route sampler against counts (mean over five station draws).',['Station coverage','Stations','Constraint','Calibrated vehicles','Fit edges GEH<5','Held-out GEH<5 (± SD)','Held-out mean GEH','Held-out r'],rowsC,widths=[0.9,0.6,1.0,0.8,0.7,1.0,0.7,0.6],fs=8.5,note='Evaluation uses hourly entering counts in the two demand hours on edges with non-zero observed or simulated flow; the uncalibrated row is evaluated on all such edge-hours.')
fig('fig/f15_calib.png','Calibration twin. (Left) Held-out edge-hours with GEH < 5 against station coverage, with counts only (blue) and with total demand added (green); dots: draws; bars: means. (Centre, right) Simulated against "observed" hourly counts for the uncalibrated demand and for a calibration with 25% station coverage.',w=6.5)
H('6.12 Cross-version and repository reproducibility',2)
para(f'Regenerating the demand with the repository parameters and the newer SUMO release produced an identical set of 1,800 trips (origins, destinations and departure times), and the mean travel time of the regenerated run (93.37 s) equals that of the stored route file (93.36 s). Running the same route files with SUMO 1.20.0 gave identical route lengths but systematically different driving outcomes in the stable regime: at p = 2 s the mean travel time was {dd.mean():.2f} s higher (95% interval {dd.mean()-ci(dd):.2f} to {dd.mean()+ci(dd):.2f} s, {dd.mean()/W["dur"]["1.28.0"].xs(2,level="p").mean()*100:.1f}%), mean time loss {dtl.mean():.2f} s higher, mean trip speed {abs(dsp.mean()):.2f} m/s lower and mean waiting time {dwa.mean():+.2f} s different (Figure 16). The paired standard deviation is only {dd.std(ddof=1):.2f} s, so the offset is systematic and not noise. At p = 1 s the two versions diverge irregularly because outcomes depend on whether a jam locks. Results are therefore tied to a SUMO version and should not be compared across releases without re-running the baseline.')
