# ------------ 7 DISCUSSION
H('7. Discussion',1)
para('Four observations follow from the results. First, the synthetic baseline behaves plausibly but is shaped more by the generator and the extraction than by the intended road. With the fringe weighting used here, three quarters of vehicles never touch the arterial, and a 13.5 km primary edge is unreachable, so the simulated system is essentially a residential district whose speeds are governed by the 50 km/h limit and junction conflicts. Reporting aggregate speed for such a network as if it described an arterial corridor would be misleading, and the connectivity audit is a cheap, general safeguard that should precede any extract-based study.')
para('Second, the demand level matters more than any single behavioural assumption near the congestion transition, and the transition is not smooth. A single seed at p = 1 s suggested a moderate deterioration, while ten seeds revealed a bifurcation between recovery and sustained gridlock, with variability two orders of magnitude larger than in the stable regime. Studies that choose demand arbitrarily, or that run one realisation per scenario, can therefore mistake a lucky or unlucky seed for an effect. A demand sweep with an ensemble, as in Experiments A and D, is cheap in SUMO and should be a default.')
para('Third, downstream modelling choices interact with the regime. Static default signals reduce the severity of gridlock at high demand but add delay at moderate demand, and an AV share reduces delay only where the baseline is stable. Indicative CO2 per kilometre was insensitive to both the AV share and, within the stable regimes, to demand, so conclusions about emission benefits need a congested baseline, a calibrated fleet and powertrain-specific emission classes. These findings are specific to the assumptions in Sections 5.8 and 5.9 and should be read as a demonstration of the analysis pipeline rather than as estimates for Riyadh.')
para('Fourth, calibration is the binding constraint. In the twin experiment route-sampler calibration with counts alone gave held-out agreement below that of an uncalibrated uniform demand at every coverage level tested, while fitted stations looked excellent, which is the signature of over-fitting to sparse constraints and of an unconstrained total demand. Supplying the known hourly totals removed much of the penalty. Counts should therefore cover a large share of the network, be accompanied by an independent total-demand estimate, and be judged on held-out stations. The cross-version offset of about 3% in travel time, although small, is larger than the width of the ten-seed intervals at moderate demand, so a calibration performed under one release would carry a bias under another. For the Riyadh setting this argues for requesting counts from the source authors or the Ministry of Transport at as many locations as possible, fixing the SUMO version used for calibration and archiving it with the data.')
para('The TraCI-derived outputs add what summary statistics cannot: the location of delay. Because two of the 250 passenger edges account for 40% of slow-speed samples, the analysis identifies the junction approaches where a signalling, priority or lane-design change would matter, and it provides the trajectory format that an AV-behaviour layer, emission model or V2X emulation would consume. Per-vehicle speed–time traces are the typical input to vehicle-activity-based emission tools, so the log written here can serve that purpose after calibration.')
# ------------ 8 LIMITATIONS
H('8. Limitations and how they were addressed',1)
para('The first version of this study listed eight limitations. Table 18 records how each was addressed in the present version. Six were eliminated or substantially reduced by new experiments, and the remaining constraints are stated in Section 8.1.')
table('Initial limitations and their treatment.',['Initial limitation','Action taken','Outcome','Status'],[
['Demand synthetic and uncalibrated','Calibration twin with route sampler (Section 6.11); data audit (Section 4)','Pipeline tested; sparse counts found insufficient; real counts still required','Reduced'],
['Network is not the target corridor','Connectivity audit and arterial stress test (Sections 6.2, 6.5)','Extract characterised and mis-scope declared; correct extract not yet built','Remains (declared)'],
['No signals in the network','Static signals at 5 and 61 junctions (Section 6.8)','Effect quantified at two demand regimes; plans untuned','Reduced'],
['Single vehicle type; no AV, V2X or emissions','Mixed fleet, AV share 0–50% and HBEFA CO2 (Section 6.9)','AV approximated by parameters; emissions indicative; V2X not modelled','Reduced'],
['Single realisation in experiments A and B','All sensitivity experiments run with 10 seeds (Sections 6.6, 6.7)','Revealed that a single seed misleads at p = 1 s','Eliminated'],
['One hour with uniform demand','Four-hour illustrative profile (Section 6.10)','Peak and recovery behaviour shown; profile not observed','Reduced'],
['Data audit limited to public pages','Not changed','Formal requests needed','Remains'],
['Results from SUMO 1.28.0 only','Cross-version test with 1.20.0 (Section 6.12)','Systematic 3% offset found and reported','Reduced']],widths=[1.7,2.0,2.0,0.8],fs=8.5)
H('8.1 Residual limitations',2)
bullets([
 'No observed counts, speeds or travel times enter the model, so no claim of realism is made; the twin experiment shows only that the calibration procedure works on simulated observations.',
 'The extract is not the corridor investigated in [10,11] and is fragmented; results must not be read as describing Prince Mohammed Ibn Salman Ibn Abdulaziz Road or Riyadh traffic. Rebuilding the extract with the correct bounding box and a connectivity check is the first task of any follow-up.',
 'Signal plans are SUMO defaults and are neither optimised nor Riyadh-specific; the finding that signals help or hurt depends on the demand regime and on timing.',
 'AV behaviour is approximated by car-following parameters, with several parameter values assumed; vehicle-to-everything communication, cooperative manoeuvres and battery-electric powertrains are not modelled, and CO2 is indicative.',
 'The time profile is illustrative; the fleet mix, signal timing and vehicle parameters are assumptions pending local statistics.',
 'The audit reports what was retrievable from public pages; data may be available through formal requests.',
 'Results are tied to the two SUMO releases tested; other releases may differ.'])
# ------------ 9 CONCLUSIONS
H('9. Conclusions',1)
para('This study provides an open, seeded baseline for SUMO and TraCI studies of Riyadh and a transparent account of its behaviour under controlled extensions. The main conclusions are as follows.')
bullets([
 'Network geometry is obtainable from OpenStreetMap, but observed demand could not be retrieved from public Riyadh sources and must be requested or synthesised.',
 'The extracted network is fragmented into seven strongly connected components and contains a 13.5 km primary edge unreachable by traffic; connectivity should be audited before use.',
 f'The baseline is stable across seeds (travel-time CV {p2.std(ddof=1)/p2.mean()*100:.1f}% at p = 2 s), but halving the insertion period to 1 s causes a bifurcation into sustained gridlock in {nTele} of {nT} seeds, so ensembles are essential.',
 f'Static default signals at five hotspot junctions reduced gridlock severity but added {h2.dur.mean()-n2.dur.mean():.0f} s of mean travel time at moderate demand; targeted placement beats blanket signalisation.',
 f'An AV share of 50% reduced mean time loss by {tl_red2:.0f}–{tl_red15:.0f}% in stable regimes without changing indicative CO2 per kilometre.',
 'Route-sampler calibration with counts alone underperformed an uncalibrated demand in the twin test; counts on many edges, a total-demand constraint and held-out checks are needed.',
 'Results shift by about 3% between SUMO 1.20.0 and 1.28.0, so the version must be fixed and reported.'])
para('Immediate next steps are to re-extract the bounding box that contains Prince Mohammed Ibn Salman Ibn Abdulaziz Road with a connectivity check, obtain counts from the source authors or the Ministry of Transport, calibrate with routeSampler.py using GEH and travel-time criteria on held-out stations, and then repeat the signal, AV and emission experiments on the calibrated baseline with local fleet statistics.')
